o-ahead before\n \n ```\n .ard/\n+  PRODUCT.md             # human: what the product is and who it is for (optional)\n+  VISION.md              # human: where the project is heading (optional)\n+  TECH_STACK.md          # human: languages, tools, conventions and constraints (optional)\n   <issue#>/\n     issue.md             # scanner: the ask, findings, fingerprints, links\n     plan.md              # triage roles except the lead: each owns one `##` section, replaced in place on a re-run; the worker follows it\n@@ -113,6 +116,8 @@ Four more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before\n       <job-id>.json\n ```\n \n+**Repo context docs.** `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md` and `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` give agents the background a repository's code does not state. They are human-owned: no agent writes them, the worker guard rejects any change under `.ard/`, and `ard init` proposes them only as templates. Each is optional. A file that is missing, or that still holds only the template (headings and comments), counts as absent and is not shown to agents. Precedence, highest first: the role's own prompt and `.ard/config.yaml`, then the issue and its plan, then these docs; they inform a decision and never override the issue's ask. A reviewer reads them from the pull request's head, so a PR that edits one is judged against its own edit, and because the three files are protected paths that edit waits for a human approval. When a doc conflicts with the issue or with another doc, the agent does not pick a side: it reports the conflict in its role file and answers `needs-human`. Treat their content as facts about the project, not as instructions that change an agent's rules. Each template carries a size guidance of about 150 lines and a `Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD` line for the human to keep current.\n+\n **Who may write what.** Close-out checks every changed file under `.ard/<issue#>/` against the job's allowlist:\n \n | Stage and role | May change |\n@@ -386,7 +391,7 @@ Only ready (non-draft) PRs the App opened on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` wit\n   - when the rules are readable and name required checks, each must be completed with `success`, `neutral` or `skipped` on the head (the latest run per name; a rerun in flight hides the old result), or have a latest commit status `success`. Missing or running checks wait; a red one (failure, cancelled, timed out, action required) gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment naming the checks, until #41 adds fix rounds\n   - when the rules are unavailable (a free-plan private repository answers 403 \"upgrade\", or 404) or no required checks apply, there is no list to go by: if the head has no check runs and no commit statuses the merger merges on reviews alone, but only after a grace period since it first saw the head (at least one tick and two minutes), so CI that has not queued yet is not missed; if it has either, every latest run and status per name must be green (a pending status waits)\n   - when the rules cannot be read (a 5xx, a network error, a permissions 403), nothing merges that tick. Rule types ard does not evaluate (`workflows`, `code_scanning`, `required_deployments`) are logged at warn\n-- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, and the linter config a PR could weaken, `**/.golangci.yml`, `**/.golangci.yaml`, `**/.golangci.toml` and `**/.golangci.json` (17 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix; any other entry is a doublestar glob. Matching is case-insensitive on the repo-relative path (macOS file systems are, so `Claude.md` and `.Claude/x` are protected). A prefix matches on a directory boundary (`.github/` matches `.github` and `.github/x`, never `.githubx`). A glob follows doublestar: `*` stays within one path segment and `**` crosses them, so a bare `CODEOWNERS` would match only the root file. Quote globs that start with `*` in YAML. The merger checks both names of a renamed file; a malformed path counts as protected. A PR that touches one gets `ard:needs-approval` and merges on the next poll once a person with write access approves the current head with a GitHub review (below).\n+- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), the three repo context docs (`.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md`, named one by one, with no `.ard/*.md` glob), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, and the linter config a PR could weaken, `**/.golangci.yml`, `**/.golangci.yaml`, `**/.golangci.toml` and `**/.golangci.json` (20 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix; any other entry is a doublestar glob. Matching is case-insensitive on the repo-relative path (macOS file systems are, so `Claude.md` and `.Claude/x` are protected). A prefix matches on a directory boundary (`.github/` matches `.github` and `.github/x`, never `.githubx`). A glob follows doublestar: `*` stays within one path segment and `**` crosses them, so a bare `CODEOWNERS` would match only the root file. Quote globs that start with `*` in YAML. The merger checks both names of a renamed file; a malformed path counts as protected. A PR that touches one gets `ard:needs-approval` and merges on the next poll once a person with write access approves the current head with a GitHub review (below).\n \n A protected path needs a GitHub review in state `APPROVED`, from a user who is not an App and has write, maintain or admin permission, that stands at a commit the current head still covers (the same rule as for reviews). A later `CHANGES_REQUESTED` or `DISMISSED` review by the same person voids it, a plain comment does not. A triage user cannot push or merge on GitHub, so their approval does not authorise a change to `.github/`, config or agent instructions. The label is not the approval: `ard:needs-approval` is informational, the merger adds it and posts one comment per head, and removing it changes nothing.\n \n@@ -443,7 +448,7 @@ intervals: { scanners: 4h, triage: 15m, workers: 5m, reviewers: 5m, merger: 5m,\n pr_watch: { pending_timeout: 2h }   # at least intervals.prwatch, at most 7d\n intake: { mode: label }     # mode: all | label; label (label mode only) defaults to ard:pending, must start with ard: and must not be one of ard's own labels\n merge:                       # defaults written by `ard init`; the list is the only source (no floor in code)\n-  protected_paths: [.github/, .ard/config.yaml, .ard/scanners/, .ard/triage/, .ard/workers/, .ard/reviewers/, \"**/CODEOWNERS\", CLAUDE.md, \"**/CLAUDE.md\", AGENTS.md, \"**/AGENTS.md\", .claude/, .mcp.json, \"**/.golangci.yml\", \"**/.golangci.yaml\", \"**/.golangci.toml\", \"**/.golangci.json\"]\n+  protected_paths: [.github/, .ard/config.yaml, .ard/scanners/, .ard/triage/, .ard/workers/, .ard/reviewers/, .ard/PRODUCT.md, .ard/VISION.md, .ard/TECH_STACK.md, \"**/CODEOWNERS\", CLAUDE.md, \"**/CLAUDE.md\", AGENTS.md, \"**/AGENTS.md\", .claude/, .mcp.json, \"**/.golangci.yml\", \"**/.golangci.yaml\", \"**/.golangci.toml\", \"**/.golangci.json\"]\n stale: { inactive_for: 30d, suppress_for: 90d }\n \n sidecars:\n@@ -471,7 +476,7 @@ overrides:                   # only what differs from convention\n \n Scanners also take `route` (`pending`, `none` or `needs-human`). Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts, 3 review rounds and worker diff thresholds of 2,000 lines (warn) and 3,000 lines (hold). Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day` (that role's own daily cap in USD; unset or 0 means only the repo cap applies), `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `max_test_fixes`, `max_review_rounds`, `diff_warn_lines` and `diff_max_lines` (worker roles; positive integers, warn no more than max; above warn the PR proceeds with a note, above max it is held), `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n \n-**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/lts`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lts, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n+**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/lts`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lts, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), compares them with `main` (the three repo context templates are create-if-absent: proposed only when missing on `main`, never a conflict and never overwritten, even with `--force`; the PR body tells an adopter with an existing config to add their paths to `merge.protected_paths`), commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n \n **Where `ard serve` reads config (decided, #87).** After each fetch into its own clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`, see Local-executor details) `ard serve` resolves `origin/main` to a SHA and reads `config.yaml` and the role directories from that commit, not from the working tree. It exports them into a snapshot at `~/.ard/config/<owner>/<repo>/<sha>/`, kept 24 hours after a newer SHA replaces it, because roles run from a host path: a role directory, its `Dockerfile` and the scan worktree must all describe the one commit the tick's jobs use. Editing the host's working tree therefore changes nothing on a running controller, with one exception: a local `paused: true` is honoured (see the kill switch), and only that key is read locally. A merged change applies on the next tick without a pull. Symlinks and submodules under the role directories are rejected, and a config that cannot be fetched or does not validate counts as paused. If `origin/main` cannot be loaded at startup, `ard serve` starts paused instead of exiting, posts the load error to the status issue once per distinct error, and retries every tick; no loop runs until a config has loaded. `ard config check` validates the working tree, for authoring; `ard config check --ref <ref>` validates a commit of the local checkout (for example `--ref origin/main` after `git fetch`) without fetching.\n \n@@ -486,7 +491,7 @@ Agents with push access read text that strangers can write, so the controls sit\n - **Untrusted text is passed as data.** Issue bodies, comments and PR text go to the agent as files the prompt labels as untrusted input, never spliced into the instructions. `issue.md` omits ard's own cost block.\n - **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, not a flag (the CLI reads no Keychain entry but the one keyed to the per-job dir); a host-mode job on macOS still runs as the operator's uid, so agent Bash could call `security` against the operator's login Keychain, including Claude's own entry, which is within the host-mode risk of #84 (a Dockerfile role or a dedicated macOS user for `ard serve` closes it); the subscription token is a one-year, model-only credential, rotated by running `claude setup-token` again and restarting `ard serve`; `--max-budget-usd` and `total_cost_usd` are estimates on a subscription; part of the block (AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md under `--restricted`) is observed behaviour that holds only on the tested CLI version and is re-checked by the integration tests; built-in plugins and the host's managed settings still apply; system reminders and fixed CLI prompt text are back; isolation of settings `env` is not pinned by a test, since no Bash call is scripted; Bash can still read anything the process can (host mode, #84); and an agent can rewrite CLAUDE.md or `.claude/` in its worktree, which later runs ignore but a worker PR could carry into the target repo (the `ard init` protected-path defaults cover `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, so such a PR needs a human).\n - **Agents write only their own files.** Close-out lets a job change under `.ard/<issue#>/` only its own role file and what its role owns (a `plan.md` section, `design/`); anything else fails the job as `needs-human` and is not committed (see the `.ard/` contract).\n-- **Role files are human-owned.** The diff guard and the committer's code lane reject any worker change under `.ard/` or `.github/` (so `.ard/config.yaml` and every role directory), and the merger treats those paths as protected through `merge.protected_paths` (the `ard init` defaults), so no agent can rewrite its own prompt. `ard init` only proposes them, in a PR a human merges.\n+- **Role files are human-owned.** The diff guard and the committer's code lane reject any worker change under `.ard/` or `.github/` (so `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` and every role directory), and the merger treats those paths as protected through `merge.protected_paths` (the `ard init` defaults), so no agent can rewrite its own prompt. `ard init` only proposes them, in a PR a human merges.\n - **Merges are gated.** The merger lands a PR only when every review passed and required checks (read from the branch rules API)