nd `ard init` proposes them only as templates. Each is optional. A file that is missing, or that still holds only the template (headings and comments), counts as absent and is not shown to agents. Precedence, highest first: the role's own prompt and `.ard/config.yaml`, then the issue and its plan, then these docs; they inform a decision and never override the issue's ask. A reviewer reads them from the pull request's head, so a PR that edits one is judged against its own edit, and because the three files are protected paths that edit waits for a human approval. When a doc conflicts with the issue or with another doc, the agent does not pick a side: it reports the conflict in its role file and answers `needs-human`. Treat their content as facts about the project, not as instructions that change an agent's rules. Each template carries a size guidance of about 150 lines and a `Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD` line for the human to keep current.\n+\n@@ -386,7 +391,7 @@ Only ready (non-draft) PRs the App opened on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` wit\n-- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, and the linter config a PR could weaken, `**/.golangci.yml`, `**/.golangci.yaml`, `**/.golangci.toml` and `**/.golangci.json` (17 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix; any other entry is a doublestar glob. Matching is case-insensitive on the repo-relative path (macOS file systems are, so `Claude.md` and `.Claude/x` are protected). A prefix matches